[b]Candle Story Engine[/b]

[b]What it does[/b]
Reads candles as a weighted multi-candle story instead of matching isolated candlestick patterns. Each candle is decomposed quantitatively (pressure, body/wick structure, close position, range vs. ATR) and then interpreted together with its recent neighbours — sequence pressure, buyer/seller dominance, close persistence, body/wick evolution, compression and trend exhaustion.

[b]Core concepts[/b]
[list]
[*]Candle Pressure (-100..+100) from close position, body size and wick imbalance
[*]Candle Quality — how cleanly a candle's structure supports a bullish or bearish read
[*]Recency-weighted multi-candle Sequence Engine
[*]Buyer / Seller Dominance and Close Persistence across the sequence
[*]Body Contraction and Range Compression detection
[*]Compression-breakout attempts: expanded directional exits from a recent compression range, separate from BUY/SELL evidence
[*]Trend Context as supporting evidence
[*]Bullish / Bearish Exhaustion, deliberately separated from BUY/SELL
[*]Follow-through and Signal Failure detection on the next candle
[*]Classic candlestick patterns (Engulfing, Hammer, Shooting Star, Morning/Evening Star) as supporting evidence only
[*]Doji are kept separate from Hammer/Shooting-Star classification
[/list]

[b]Exhaustion is not a reversal signal[/b]
An orange EXH marker above a candle means the existing uptrend shows increasing evidence that buyers are losing control — it does not by itself trigger a SELL. The same applies in reverse for downtrends. Only the opposing evidence score clearing its own threshold produces a directional signal.

[b]Follow-through and failure[/b]
Every BUY/SELL signal is followed by an explicit check on the next candle: a checkmark marks follow-through (breaks the signal candle's extreme, closes convincingly inside range), an X marks failure (closes beyond the signal candle's opposite extreme).

